% HOW TO UPDATE THIS FILE
%
% - Add/replace entries by pasting BibTeX exported from Google Scholar (click the
%   quote-mark "Cite" icon on a paper -> "BibTeX"), or from any other reference
%   manager. You can keep everything in this one file, or drop additional .bib
%   files into this same folder (src/data/publications/) - all *.bib files here
%   are read automatically.
% - Two custom (non-standard BibTeX) fields are supported on any entry:
%     category = {journal|conference|book|preprint|unpublished|other}
%       Controls which section/filter an entry appears under. If you omit it,
%       the BibTeX entry type (article/inproceedings/book/etc.) is used to guess
%       a reasonable category.
%     pdf = {https://...}
%       A personal/self-hosted "draft copy" of the paper (a Google Drive share,
%       a lab-hosted PDF, a ResearchGate mirror, etc.), shown as a separate
%       "Draft paper" link below the citation - always distinct from the
%       official published version.
% - The standard `doi = {10.xxxx/yyyy}` BibTeX field (just the bare DOI, not a
%   URL) is what drives the official "published version" link shown below each
%   citation - it's turned into a canonical https://doi.org/<doi> link and
%   labeled with the matching repository (IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library,
%   etc.) automatically. Paste it in exactly as Google Scholar's BibTeX export
%   gives it to you. An entry with no `doi` field shows only its `pdf` draft
%   link, if any - never a non-DOI publisher URL.
% - `url` is kept in sync with the DOI link automatically for entries that
%   have one; it isn't something you need to set by hand for those. It still
%   matters for entries with no DOI (the two unpublished-category entries
%   below use it directly as their download link).
% - Author lists should be the real, correctly-ordered co-author list from a
%   Google Scholar BibTeX export - Brandon's name is bolded automatically
%   wherever it falls in that list (first author, middle, or last), so there's
